On Dec 17, 8:03 pm, David Ploog <pl...@mi.fu-berlin.de> wrote:

> Now the fact is that there is no urgency. The quest leader will happily
> wait another 100000 turns until you arrive. For some classes, postponing
> the quest as far as possible is generally advised.

It's in keeping with the principle that nothing happens on a level
while you're not on it. (Except that corpses and light sources still
manage to age, and pets manage to keep track of how long you haven't
been around.)

> Another flaw is that
> the Quest text describes a battle, but attackers and defenders will
> peacefully walk by each other.

Just an expression of a meta-flaw that the player is the only thing
that non-pet monsters will ever take the initiative in fighting, when
conflict isn't involved. Why is there no mechanism for having certain
monsters want to fight each other on general principle? E.g., purple
worms are attracted to the sound of shriekers because the worms find
the shriekers delicious...but you've never seen a worm eat the
shrieker that called it without being tamed or conflicted, right?

> My proposal:
> (1) Apply some sort of the grudge patch to Home:1. It is not ideal, as it
> only causes combat for adjacent beings. So additional AI would be helpful.

Which addresses the above issue, at least for one level.

> (2) Track the player's effort in rescuing the Quest leader and his
> friends. This could be done by counting (and weighting properly)
> * number of turns between getting the message and entering portal,

Only caveat to this is that you get the occasional game where you end
up on the portal level well before you could possibly be prepared to
do so -- one unlucky trapdoor on or around DL 10 would do the trick.
However, as you say later, you're never *penalized* for not doing Home:
1 as a speedrun, so this is only of minor concern. Some things just
aren't meant to happen every game -- ask anyone who needs to buy
protection late in the game who doesn't have Ludios in their dungeon.

> * number of turns spent on Home:1 (until you meet the Leader) [1],
> * number of casualties on your faction,

Add a small penalty for friendly fire deaths. (Expert cone of cold or
fireball may prove hazardous here.)

> * number of enemies killed.

Maybe I've been playing too much Metal Gear Solid lately, but a small
bonus per enemy that you immobilize/incapacitate non-lethally, or
scare off the level? Taking prisoners or driving the foes off is an
equally valid objective, and can be more challenging than simply
killing them.

Also perhaps a small penalty for enemies that you see but don't deal
with in some way, so that it's not all about simply sprinting to the
Leader. Even a token effort (fling one rock) would erase this flag for
each enemy. It would not come into play for enemies generated "behind"
you (relative to you, further from the Leader) after you've entered
the level.

> [1] When evaluating quickness on Home:1, the numbers must depend on the
> class in question. For example, the Ranger quest takes notoriously long to
> finish (and is hard), many others are much quicker.

Hypothetically, you could generate the shortest walking path from the
portal to the leader and base your turncount scale on that. You can do
a pretty good sprint on some quest levels; Ran is a heck of a slog,
and there's little recourse for dealing with the enemies except to
kill them.

> This should create several choices:
> (a) Do you want to go for the reward? This might contain a risk, and the
> loss of resources.

That's key. It's optional and has a reasonably substantial reward, or
the potential thereof, so you need to choose between spending the
effort to go for the reward, or skipping it.

> (d) Note that nothing changes for players opting to ignore all of this.

In the grand tradition of providing subtle clues to the unspoiled, you
may want to hand out a "booby prize" anyhow to someone who takes an
excessive amount of time to report to the Leader. "It's been so long
since I called for you, I feared that you might never show up. As you
can see, the <nemesis> left us with very little, but we are not
completely without means to succor you." ... and hand over a potion of
water, or a slime mold. Just some sort of hint that you'll want to
perhaps hurry it up a bit more the next time around. And it's
something that is not completely useless, but it's not a game-breaker
to get a freebie. (No potions of fruit juice, for instance -- you then
get a free ID of fruit juice, effectively.)

> * It might be an option to have Home:1 change depending on how late you
> enter. This could be visuals only: buildings (more and more) broken etc.

A sliding scale, based on turncount, applied to every wall square,
with chances of converting them to boulder/rock pile/empty square/pit?
You'd need to tweak it a little for things like the Cav quest, where
there are no buildings.


